# ro.vendor.arcfox.dt2w -- the double-tap-to-wake switch read by
# init.arcfox-touch-gesture.rc. It gets a type of its own so that shell can READ
# it (`adb shell getprop`), which the vendor_default_prop fallthrough would not
# allow: property.te:189 neverallows a coredomain from reading any vendor
# property that is not vendor_public_property_type.
#
# It is NOT shell-writable and cannot be made so -- see property.te for the
# neverallow that rules it out.
#
# Deliberately NOT folded into the existing persist.vendor.touch. prefix
# (vendor_mot_touchscreen_prop): that type is stock's, is written by
# /vendor/bin/init.mmi.touch.sh, and this switch has nothing to do with the
# touch firmware calibration properties that live there.
# ro.vendor.arcfox.dt2w deliberately has NO custom context: measured on device,
# vendor props carrying a custom type were silently DROPPED at boot (vendor_init
# has no set permission for it), while ro.vendor.* props with no explicit context
# (e.g. ro.vendor.beluga.*) load fine and are still readable by shell.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROUND TWO -- see property.te for the measurement behind each of these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ro.sys.battery.FirstUsageDate and ro.rkp.csr_uploaded ARE NOT HERE, AND CANNOT
# BE. The build enforces the vendor property namespace:
#
#   check_prop_prefix --strict ... 2 violations found:
#     ro.sys.battery.FirstUsageDate   u:object_r:vendor_mot_battinfo_prop:s0
#     ro.rkp.csr_uploaded             u:object_r:vendor_mot_init_prop:s0
#   vendor's and odm's property_contexts MUST use ONLY vendor-prefixed
#   properties.  (allowed: ro.vendor. ro.odm. ro.boot. ro.hardware. vendor.
#   odm. persist.vendor. persist.odm. ctl.vendor. init.svc.vendor. ...)
#
# Both are labelled exported_default_prop in
# ../system_ext/private/property_contexts instead. See that file for why no new
# type is declared.

# Set by /vendor/bin/init.oem.hw.sh from the esimid UTAG. It is NOT covered by
# the ro.vendor.hw. prefix above -- the script writes BOTH ro.vendor.hw.esimid
# (which lands on vendor_mot_hw_prop and works) and the bare ro.vendor.esimid
# (which fell through to default_prop and was refused):
#
#   init: Unable to set property 'ro.vendor.esimid' from uid:0 gid:0 pid:1196:
#         SELinux permission check failed
#   init.oem.hw.sh: ro.vendor.esimid='8903302355342901...'
#
# init_hw already holds set_prop on vendor_mot_init_prop (init_hw.te:131), so
# this one line is the whole fix. Type is stock's (vendor_property_contexts:444).
#
# VERIFIED WORKING AFTER FLASHING -- AND `adb shell getprop ro.vendor.esimid`
# PRINTS NOTHING ANYWAY. Do not read that as the fix having failed; it was
# reported as such once already.
#
# What the post-flash capture shows: the "Unable to set property
# 'ro.vendor.esimid' ... SELinux permission check failed" line is GONE and there
# is no AVC for it. What `getprop` shows is a SHELL READ failure, caught live:
#
#   $ adb shell getprop ro.vendor.esimid          -> (empty)
#   $ adb shell getprop ro.vendor.hw.esimid       -> 8903302355342901...
#   avc: denied { read } for name="u:object_r:vendor_mot_init_prop:s0"
#        scontext=u:r:shell:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_mot_init_prop:s0
#        tclass=file
#
# Both properties are written by the same script, from the same UTAG, in the
# same run; the only difference is the type. property.te grants shell read on
# vendor_mot_hw_prop and NOT on vendor_mot_init_prop, and getprop prints an
# empty string for a property it cannot read rather than an error. All six
# vendor_mot_init_prop properties (esimid, bootreason, boot_seq,
# manufacturedate, mot.iccid, mot.cust_md5) read empty from shell for this
# reason.
#
# NOT WIDENED TO shell, on purpose. Stock's readers of vendor_mot_init_prop are
# apanic, charge_only, init_display, init_hw, init_usb, mmi_attest_chk,
# mmi_boot, surfaceflinger, tcmd, vendor_init and vendor_thermal-engine -- shell
# is not among them, and the value is an eSIM ICCID. Our policy already matches
# stock (all the vendor domains via `{ domain -coredomain -appdomain }`, plus
# surfaceflinger and dumpstate). Verify it from dumpstate/a bugreport, or by
# reading its twin ro.vendor.hw.esimid; nothing in this ROM consumes the bare
# name (grepped the whole built image: the only hit is this file).
ro.vendor.esimid                u:object_r:vendor_mot_init_prop:s0

